7] [17592186060678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060679 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060679 #edb/ref 1061 17173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c9a13d7-cb68-5563-a0cd-305ad4cad7f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060680 #edb/ref 1061 17174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6cdbf1b-5a64-5b3d-b66b-3ba956756491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060681 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060681 #edb/ref 1061 17183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060681 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3df97c2e-febb-5f72-be71-e1d739dbbd92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060681 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060682 #edb/ref 1061 17184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46eeafc2-7d22-58a7-bc12-127e3c86f554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060683 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060683 #edb/ref 1061 17185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060683 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "797229e4-e202-5744-a0d3-6e8c4cae4059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060683 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060684 #edb/ref 1061 17186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cfe1d425-2185-5011-8249-58d7c6bd7ec8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060685 #edb/ref 1061 17187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0daa33bf-3f45-5650-9a9c-808f765e3f26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060938 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use matrices to solve systems of linear equations. To do this, we need to set up the **augmented matrix** for the system.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=-1 \\\\ -3x+7y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the **coefficient matrix**, which consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we create the **augmented matrix**. This is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the future, we will learn how to use coefficient and augmented matrices to solve systems of equations. But for now, let's practice setting them up." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060938 #edb/ref 1065 3146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060938 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "36b1a271-77b9-59ed-9f12-1b6675c26830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060938 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060939 #edb/ref 1064 "We will use mostly systems with the following variables:\n\n- $x,y,z,$ or\n\n- $x_1,x_2,x_3,$ or\n\n- $a,b,c.$\n\nThese sets of variables will always have a *natural* order:\n\n- alphabetical order in the case of $x,y,z$ or $a,b,c,$ and\n\n- numerical order in the case of $x_1, x_2, x_3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060939 #edb/ref 1065 3147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060939 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f6081e68-9014-5a0d-a6bf-084abe5fd28e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060939 #edb/ref 1067 "The Order of the Variables in a Linear System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060940 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060940 #edb/ref 1065 3150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060940 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7aa753a7-1450-53dd-8e61-71e07952cecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060940 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084548 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0c68cfab-095b-58cc-9da7-ba0916b77f5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084549 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\frac{dy}{dx} - x^{2}y = x\\sin x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c13a4f93-e1f9-5884-a39c-c79a4bcb3e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084550 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\frac{e^{x}}{x}y = x^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3444d68-c1e5-5947-a906-9f7391cdd707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084551 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 4xy = \\sqrt{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084551 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084551 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6b4eab8-6e07-5ae7-96d3-a9034a47b11e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084552 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx} - x^{2} = \\frac{1}{1 - y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084552 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084552 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f0bf6cc-cd01-5352-adcb-7f989d4f9813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084553 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{dy}{dx} - (\\tan x)y = \\sec x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084553 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "511a9ab3-97fa-5de3-a077-f939d6fb35ab" #edb/ref 1