592186397780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087867 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087867 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087867 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087868 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to First-Order Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087962 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "22d6d34d-749f-599b-a696-f6952b1b29b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087969 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **NOT** a first order linear ordinary differential equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d7eb342-85c3-52e0-b57f-d62a1be729ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088797 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088797 #edb/ref 1061 593007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cc3efa3-9929-5b62-9be5-629d74b8499c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088798 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088798 #edb/ref 1061 593008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088798 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95fd0f61-4e3f-5214-a406-a8309198f92d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088798 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088799 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088799 #edb/ref 1061 593009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088799 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e7cbc14-094f-5a40-a91a-a3b62a3ad64b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088799 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088800 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088800 #edb/ref 1061 593012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088800 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29f2610f-f30f-5b70-bb79-098ffb891c67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088800 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088801 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088801 #edb/ref 1061 593016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088801 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0d57580-3bcd-5a38-92ff-b1a7d2279815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088801 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088802 #edb/ref 1061 593017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088802 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "685e843b-9372-5936-b5c3-cc142d209a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088802 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088862 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the standard form of a first-order linear differential equation is\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that $Q(x)$ is called the **forcing function.**\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **homogeneous** if the forcing function $Q(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ In other words, the equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **inhomogeneous** if it is not homogeneous.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 0.\n$$\n\nThe equation matches the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = -x.$ Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Now, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 6.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $Q(x) = 6$ means that the equation cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is *not* homogeneous (i.e., it is *inhomogeneous*).\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + xy = 2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left-hand side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+xy & =2y \\\\ {y}^{'}+xy-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{'}+(x-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = (x-2).$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088862 #edb/ref 1065 14653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088862 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2107c0cc-b904-547f-b13d-9a0898b33d73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088862 #edb/ref 1067 "Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088944 #edb/ref 1064 "A **first-order linear ordinary differential equation** is a differential equation in the variable $y(x)$ that can be expressed in the **general form**\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $f(x)$ are functions of $x$ only, and\n\n- $a(x)$ is not identically zero (i.e., it is not zero for all $x$).\n\nFor example, the differential equation\n\n$$\nxy' + e^x y=1\n$$\n\nis a first-order linear ODE with $a(x) = x, b(x) = e^x,$ and $f(x) = 1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the differential equation \n\n$$\nx(y')^2 +  e^x \\sin(y)=1\n$$\n\nis *not* a first-order linear ODE. Notice that it contains the terms $(y')^2$ and $\\sin(y),$ and therefore cannot be presented in the general form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088944 #edb/ref 1065 4055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088944 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cc4983db-1d7c-5573-a6d1-9e5e4a09207e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088944 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088945 #edb/ref 1064 "The general form of a first-order linear ODE is\n\n$$\na(x)y'+b(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, if we take the general form and divide both sides by $a(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a(x)}{a(x)}{y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)} \\\\ {y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $a(x)$ is not identically zero, so we're allowed to perform this division.\n\nNow, if we define the functions $P$ and $Q$ as \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}, \\qquad Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{a(x)},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can write our equation in the following **standard form**:\n\n$$\ny'+P(x)y =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFor example, given the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\ny' \\sin x+ y e^x= \\cos x,\n$$\n\nthe standard form of this equation can be found by dividing through by $\\sin x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+y(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{sin}x}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\ {y}^{'}+y{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{cot}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nP(x) = e^x \\csc x, \\qquad Q(x) = \\cot x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088945 #edb/ref 1065 4056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088945 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d0517ea0-b60e-5c34-bfbc-41fd6c61af75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088945 #edb/ref 1067 "The Standard Form of a First-Order Linear ODE" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105768 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106298 #edb/r